text:
The disk alarm was a false one. Root usage is now going up and down, and none of our processes are writing large amounts; there's 967 GB free. Our own folders are small: 9.5 GB of build files in the repo, 0.3 GB in `/tmp`. The larger growth over the afternoon is likely Nix builds or other sessions, which I can't see from here.

Mac testing is allowed again. I've updated the saved rule: test runs on the Mac are fine because it's the only Apple Silicon Mac, as long as each run is stopped cleanly afterwards.

All eleven agents were active within the last five minutes:
- macOS VM tests;
- the preview delays;
- popup and placement;
- F1/advisor Alt targets;
- Alt-mode unit buttons, which is waiting for other agents' half-finished files to compile;
- city screen fixes;
- the hang;
- playing in the other view;
- two agents removing the locks;
- a Sonnet helper that navigates the game to the city screen.

None look stuck. Once they report, I'll run the full test suite and do the combined update to your Mac.

The screen placement setting in Layout now uses icons. Each is a small window picture showing where screens go: left or right half (or top and bottom when the split is horizontal), or a floating box. The icon follows the split direction, and each has a caption under it, like the split icons. Our settings menu has no hover text, which is why there are captions. The buttons work with the mouse and in Alt mode, and the settings tests pass.

Nobody has looked at them on screen yet. A Sonnet tester will check them during the final test run.

Still running:
- the preview delays;
- F1 and advisor Alt targets;
- the Alt recapture change;
- the hang;
- playing in the other view;
- separate fog of war per view;
- the macOS VM tests.

I've asked all ten running agents to write handover notes and then stop. Each one finishes or rolls back any step it's halfway through, leaves the code compiling, and writes its notes to `/mnt/datasets/c3r-build/handover/`. The notes cover what's done, what's in progress and whether the code compiles, next steps, how to build and test, and the rules they learned. They'll also stop their own processes on papa and on the Mac.

I've written my own overview there as `00-orchestrator.md`. It covers:
- the repo and project goal;
- all your standing rules: no compiling on the Mac, install on the Mac immediately, only Sonnet looks at screenshots, no locks, builds only on the data disk, AMD iGPU only, and the dictation words;
- what's currently installed on your Mac (the five dual-stack files) and what's finished in the code but not installed yet;
- a list of the open work, with a pointer to each agent's notes.

I'll post the full list once all ten notes have arrived.
